# TypeDoc Documentation Standards for Cloud Text-to-Speech

This rule defines the comprehensive TypeDoc documentation standards for all TypeScript code in the Cloud Text-to-Speech project.

## 🚨 MANDATORY TYPEDOC POLICY

**TypeDoc documentation MUST be created or updated every time code is written or modified.** Documentation is not optional - it is a required part of the development process that directly impacts the generated API documentation website.

### ⚠️ CRITICAL RULE: NO LOGIC CHANGES DURING DOCUMENTATION

**NEVER change the existing logic or functionality when adding or updating documentation.** The sole purpose of documentation tasks is to improve code clarity and API documentation without modifying behavior.

**Absolutely prohibited during documentation work:**
- ❌ Changing method implementations or algorithms
- ❌ Modifying function parameters or return types
- ❌ Altering class constructors or property assignments
- ❌ Adding new features or functionality
- ❌ Fixing bugs or performance issues
- ❌ Refactoring existing code structure
- ❌ Changing error handling logic
- ❌ Modifying variable names or types

**Only allowed during documentation work:**
- ✅ Adding TypeDoc comments to classes, methods, and interfaces
- ✅ Adding inline comments to explain complex logic
- ✅ Updating existing comments to match current implementation
- ✅ Adding `@example` code blocks in documentation
- ✅ Adding proper `@category`, `@param`, `@returns`, and `@throws` tags
- ✅ Improving comment formatting and readability

**Documentation work must be purely additive and descriptive - the code's behavior must remain exactly the same before and after documentation updates.**

### When TypeDoc Documentation Must Be Updated:
- ✅ **Creating new classes** - Add complete class-level TypeDoc comments with categories
- ✅ **Adding new methods/functions** - Add comprehensive method documentation with proper tags
- ✅ **Modifying existing methods** - Update TypeDoc comments to reflect changes
- ✅ **Changing method signatures** - Update parameter and return type documentation
- ✅ **Adding new interfaces/types** - Document purpose and usage with TypeDoc-specific tags
- ✅ **Refactoring code logic** - Update inline comments to match new implementation
- ✅ **Fixing bugs** - Add comments explaining the fix and prevention
- ✅ **Adding error handling** - Document new error conditions with `@throws` tags
- ✅ **Performance optimizations** - Comment on the optimization and expected impact
- ✅ **Security changes** - Document security considerations and implications

### TypeDoc-First Development:
1. **Write TypeDoc comments before implementation** - Design your API through documentation
2. **Use TypeDoc categories and modules** - Organize documentation for better navigation
3. **Include working examples** - All `@example` tags must contain runnable code
4. **Review generated docs** - Check the actual HTML output, not just the comments
5. **Test documentation examples** - Ensure code examples actually work

## TypeDoc Categories and Organization

### Module Categories
Use TypeDoc's `@category` tag to organize classes and functions:

```typescript
/**
 * Universal Text-to-Speech interface supporting multiple cloud providers
 * 
 * @category Universal API
 */
export class TtsUniversal {
}

/**
 * Google Cloud Text-to-Speech implementation
 * 
 * @category Google Cloud TTS
 */
export class TtsGoogle {
}

/**
 * Error handling utilities for TTS operations
 * 
 * @category Error Handling
 */
export class TtsError extends Error {
}
```

### Supported Categories (Must match typedoc.json configuration):
- `Universal API` - Universal/cross-provider interfaces
- `Google Cloud TTS` - Google-specific implementations
- `Microsoft Azure TTS` - Microsoft-specific implementations  
- `Amazon Polly TTS` - Amazon-specific implementations
- `Common Utilities` - Shared utility functions and helpers
- `Error Handling` - Error classes and handling utilities
- `Audio Processing` - Audio format and streaming utilities
- `Voice Management` - Voice selection and metadata
- `Configuration` - Configuration and initialization utilities

## Class Documentation Standards

### 🔄 Update Trigger: EVERY time a class is created, modified, or refactored

### Class Header Template
All classes must have TypeDoc comments with proper categorization:

```typescript
/**
 * [Brief one-line description of the class purpose]
 * 
 * [Detailed description explaining what the class does, its main responsibilities,
 * and any important implementation details or design patterns used]
 * 
 * @example Basic Usage
 * ```typescript
 * // Initialize the service
 * const tts = new ClassName(options);
 * 
 * // Perform operation
 * const result = await tts.method();
 * console.log(result);
 * ```
 * 
 * @example Advanced Usage with Error Handling
 * ```typescript
 * try {
 *   const instance = new ClassName({
 *     provider: 'google',
 *     apiKey: 'your-key'
 *   });
 *   const result = await instance.complexMethod(params);
 * } catch (error) {
 *   if (error instanceof TtsError) {
 *     console.error(`TTS Error: ${error.getUserMessage()}`);
 *   }
 * }
 * ```
 * 
 * @category [Category Name from supported list above]
 * @since [version when class was introduced]
 * @see {@link RelatedClass} for related functionality
 * @see {@link https://docs.provider.com/api | Provider API Documentation}
 */
export class ClassName {
}
```

### Class Property Documentation
Document significant class properties with TypeDoc syntax:

```typescript
/**
 * Configuration options for retry behavior
 * @readonly
 */
private readonly options: Required<RetryOptions>;

/**
 * Cache storage for voice data with automatic cleanup
 * @internal
 */
private cache = new Map<string, CacheEntry<T>>();

/**
 * Current retry attempt count
 * @defaultValue 0
 */
public retryCount: number = 0;
```

## Function/Method Documentation Standards

### 🔄 Update Trigger: EVERY time a method is created, modified, parameters changed, or return type altered

### Public Method Template
All public methods must have comprehensive TypeDoc documentation:

```typescript
/**
 * [Brief one-line description of what the method does]
 * 
 * [Detailed description explaining the method's behavior, side effects,
 * and any important implementation details]
 * 
 * @param paramName - Description of parameter, including constraints or special values
 * @param optionalParam - Description with default behavior when not provided
 * @returns Promise resolving to the result with description of possible states
 * 
 * @throws {@link TtsAuthenticationError} When API credentials are invalid or expired
 * @throws {@link TtsRateLimitError} When rate limits are exceeded
 * @throws {@link TtsValidationError} When input parameters fail validation
 * 
 * @example Basic Usage
 * ```typescript
 * const result = await instance.methodName('input text');
 * console.log(result.audio); // Uint8Array containing audio data
 * ```
 * 
 * @example With Options
 * ```typescript
 * const result = await instance.methodName('text', {
 *   voice: 'en-US-Standard-A',
 *   audioFormat: 'mp3',
 *   speed: 1.2
 * });
 * ```
 * 
 * @since 3.0.0
 * @category [Category Name]
 */
public async methodName(
  paramName: string, 
  optionalParam?: MethodOptions
): Promise<AudioResult> {
}
```

